window.SPL_SEARCH_INDEX = [{"title":"Built-ins","page":"Built-ins","href":"builtins.html","level":1,"text":"A deliberately small, Python-flavoured standard library - enough to validate, reshape and report on LLM output without leaving the language."},{"title":"Functions","page":"Built-ins","href":"builtins.html#functions","level":2,"text":""},{"title":"Types & conversion","page":"Built-ins","href":"builtins.html#conversion","level":3,"text":"SignatureDescription len(x)Length of a string (in characters), list or dict. str(x)Display form: strings as is, True/False/None, numbers plainly, lists and dicts as JSON. int(x)Truncates a number; converts True/False to 1/0; parses an integer string. float(x)Numbers pass through; booleans become 1/0; numeric strings are parsed. bool(x)Truthiness of any value. type(x)One of 'None' 'bool' 'number' '"},{"title":"Numbers & sequences","page":"Built-ins","href":"builtins.html#numbers","level":3,"text":"SignatureDescription range(stop)range(start, stop[, step])List of integers; step may be negative but not zero. abs(n)Absolute value. round(n[, digits])Without digits: rounds half to even (round(2.5) is 2). With digits: rounds to that many decimal places. min(x) / max(x)min(a, b, …)Smallest / largest of an iterable or of the arguments. Empty input is an error. sum(x)Sum of an iterable of numbers. s"},{"title":"Dicts & JSON","page":"Built-ins","href":"builtins.html#dict-fns","level":3,"text":"SignatureDescription keys(d) values(d) items(d)Lists of keys, values, or [key, value] pairs. json_parse(s)Parses a JSON string into a value. Invalid JSON raises runtime_error. json_stringify(v[, indent])Serializes to JSON, optionally pretty-printed with indent spaces."},{"title":"Logging","page":"Built-ins","href":"builtins.html#logging","level":3,"text":"SignatureDescription log(a, b, …)Writes the str() of each argument, space-separated, to stderr through the logger. Returns None. Never pollutes the result on stdout."},{"title":"Methods","page":"Built-ins","href":"builtins.html#methods","level":2,"text":"Methods are called with dot syntax on strings, lists and dicts. Strings are immutable; list and dict methods that say \"in place\" mutate the value and return None."},{"title":"String methods","page":"Built-ins","href":"builtins.html#string-methods","level":3,"text":"MethodDescription upper() lower()Change case. title() capitalize()Title-case every word / capitalize the first character. strip() lstrip() rstrip()Trim whitespace from both / left / right. split([sep])Without a separator: split on runs of whitespace. With one: split on exactly that text (empty separator is an error). splitlines()Split on \\n or \\r\\n. An empty string gives []. join(items)', '.join(p"},{"title":"List methods","page":"Built-ins","href":"builtins.html#list-methods","level":3,"text":"MethodDescription append(x)Add an item at the end (in place). extend(iterable)Append all items of another iterable (in place). insert(i, x)Insert before index i (in place). pop([i])Remove and return the item at i (default: last). remove(x)Remove the first item equal to x; error if missing (in place). index(x)Index of the first equal item, or -1. count(x)Number of equal items. sort([reverse]) rever"},{"title":"Dict methods","page":"Built-ins","href":"builtins.html#dict-methods","level":3,"text":"MethodDescription keys() values() items()Lists of keys, values, [key, value] pairs. get(key[, default])Value for key, or default (None if omitted). The safe alternative to d.key, which fails on a missing key. pop(key[, default])Remove and return a key; error if missing and no default. update(other)Merge another dict in (in place). copy() clear()Shallow copy / remove all keys. 💡 Safe access to mod"},{"title":"Putting it together","page":"Built-ins","href":"builtins.html#example","level":2,"text":""},{"title":"CLI & runtime","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html","level":1,"text":"The interpreter runs one script file to completion. It prints the result on stdout, logs on stderr and reports the outcome through its exit code."},{"title":"Usage","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#usage","level":2,"text":"From a checkout use node dist/cli.js or npx file:./lang. The package installs two equivalent binaries: spl and spl-lang. A script run expects exactly one script file; login and logout are separate commands, see Authentication."},{"title":"Options","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#options","level":2,"text":"OptionDefaultDescription --log-level <level>infoerror, info or debug. Logs go to stderr. --silentoffSuppress all logs; only the result is printed. --model <id>SDK defaultModel override. Takes precedence over @{model=…}. --store <file.json>-Persist top-level variables in a JSON file. --store-module <path>-Custom store module exporting createStore(). Mutually exclusive with --store. --timeout <secon"},{"title":"Output streams & exit codes","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#streams","level":2,"text":"stdout The value of success or of a top-level return: strings raw, everything else as pretty-printed JSON. On an uncaught failure: the failure payload. stderr Logs, log() output, syntax errors, and for every LLM call the effective model and reasoning effort. Exit codeMeaning 0Success. 1Uncaught script failure. 2Syntax or usage error. 70Internal error in the interpreter."},{"title":"Logging","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#logging","level":2,"text":"LevelShows errorErrors (for example a variable store that could not be saved), plus the full assembled prompt and raw response of every LLM call. info (default)Everything above plus warnings, one line per LLM call with its effective parameters and duration, think{show=True} reasoning, log() messages. debugEverything above plus tool invocations, caught failures and active restrictions. LLM requests"},{"title":"Variable store","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#store","level":2,"text":"Scripts can keep state between runs. --store vars.json loads top-level variables from the file at start and writes them back at exit - also on failure. Functions are not persisted. The file must contain a JSON object; writes are atomic (temp file + rename)."},{"title":"Custom stores","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#custom-store","level":3,"text":"--store-module ./mystore.mjs plugs in your own persistence. The module exports createStore() (or a default export of that shape) returning an object with load() and save(vars): ⚠️ Synchronous interface load() and save() are synchronous, so a store must complete its I/O before returning. createStore() itself may be async. save() receives plain JSON-compatible values (vars), never functions."},{"title":"Authentication","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#login","level":2,"text":"LLM calls go to GitHub Copilot, so the runtime needs credentials. Run spl login once: it does nothing if the runtime is already authenticated (an earlier login, a Copilot CLI or gh login, or COPILOT_GITHUB_TOKEN / GH_TOKEN / GITHUB_TOKEN); otherwise it starts GitHub's device flow, prints a one-time code and a URL, waits until you confirm in the browser, verifies the token with the Copilot runtime "},{"title":"Backend","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#backends","level":2,"text":"Each LLM call opens a fresh session through the GitHub Copilot SDK with a system message that frames the model as the execution engine of a script, exposing exactly five tools - read_file, write_file, web_fetch, web_search, shell - implemented by the SPL runtime so that restrictions apply. Any other permission request is denied. There is no offline backend: tests run against Copilot itself."},{"title":"Reproducibility","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#reproducibility","level":2,"text":"Control flow, arithmetic and interpolation are deterministic. For LLM calls reproducibility is best effort: The Copilot SDK has no temperature or seed setting, so the language has none either. think levels map to the SDK reasoning effort. If the selected model does not support it, a warning is logged and the model default is used. Guard against variance with assert and fmt='object'; see retry patt"},{"title":"VS Code extension","page":"VS Code extension","href":"editor.html","level":1,"text":"Syntax highlighting, snippets and editing support for .spl scripts. The extension lives in the vscode/ folder of the repository."},{"title":"Features","page":"VS Code extension","href":"editor.html#features","level":2,"text":"🎨"},{"title":"Install","page":"VS Code extension","href":"editor.html#install","level":2,"text":"For development, open the vscode/ folder in VS Code and press F5 to launch an Extension Development Host."},{"title":"Snippets","page":"VS Code extension","href":"editor.html#snippets","level":2,"text":"Type the prefix, then Tab. AreaPrefixes Control flowdef if ifelse for while let LLMask askobj think model Failurestry retry assert fail success Scopesenforce forbid restrict restrictnet restrictexec Toolsread_file write_file web_fetch web_search shell"},{"title":"Develop & test","page":"VS Code extension","href":"editor.html#develop","level":2,"text":"test/*.spl contain scope assertions: a line # ^^^ scope.name asserts the scope of the columns under the carets on the line above (- scope asserts absence). test/run-assertions.mjs runs them with vscode-textmate. test/check-examples.mjs tokenizes every script in ../lang/examples and fails on invalid scopes or on strings/blocks that are left open at the end of a file. 🔄 Keep the grammar in sync Whe"},{"title":"Examples","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html","level":1,"text":"Real scripts from lang/examples/. Run them from the lang/ folder; --cwd <dir> points a script at another project. The retry logic of triage-issues and extract-invoice is covered by tests/examples.test.ts."},{"title":"Commit message","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#commit-message","level":2,"text":"Draft a Conventional Commit message for the staged changes. The model may only run git; the result is validated deterministically and retried until it passes. 🔑Pattern: deterministic validator The model proposes; is_valid() decides. The loop gives it three tries, and the final assert makes sure a script never succeeds with an invalid message."},{"title":"Triage issues","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#triage-issues","level":2,"text":"Classify a batch of issues into labels and priorities. LLM output is treated as untrusted input: deterministic code checks it and asks again, telling the model why it was rejected."},{"title":"Extract an invoice","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#extract-invoice","level":2,"text":"Extract structured data from messy text and cross-check it with plain arithmetic. If the numbers do not add up, the rejection reason is fed back to the model for another try."},{"title":"Code review as a CI gate","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#code-review","level":2,"text":"A two-stage review: think deeply in prose, then structure the findings. The review phase may only touch ./src; the report is written under ./reports. Exit code 1 (failure payload on stdout) when high-severity findings exist."},{"title":"Release notes","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#release-notes","level":2,"text":"Generate release notes from recent git history and save them to ./out/RELEASE_NOTES.md. The model may only run git; file access is limited to ./out. Note how rules and restrictions nest."},{"title":"Web digest","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#web-digest","level":2,"text":"Summarize a web page with network access limited to a single host - and prove the sandbox holds by catching a blocked request to a second host."},{"title":"Fix failing tests","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#fix-failing-tests","level":2,"text":"An agent loop: run the suite, let the model repair the source, repeat (at most three rounds). Only npm may be executed, files are limited to the project, and tests are off limits. Every npm test run costs one LLM call, because tool output is passed through your instructions. 🔑Pattern: failure as input A failing npm test is a tool_error whose reason contains the test output. Catching it turns the "},{"title":"Translate docs","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#translate-docs","level":2,"text":"Translate a document into several languages, one file each, without leaving the project folder. The model reads the source and writes each output itself - the output path is pinned by the script."},{"title":"Journal with persistent state","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#journal","level":2,"text":"A rolling journal that remembers previous runs through the variable store. Top-level variables are saved on exit and reloaded on the next run, so state survives between runs."},{"title":"Feature tours","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#tours","level":2,"text":"Small scripts that exercise one area each."},{"title":"basics.spl - offline-friendly tour","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#basics","level":3,"text":""},{"title":"Live tours","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#live-tours","level":3,"text":"live.spl - a quick smoke test of ? with len/fmt, think, enforce and assert against the real model. tools-live.spl - read_file, write_file and think{show=True} inside restrict './sandbox'. Run it after mkdir sandbox; echo hello world > sandbox/note.txt. restrict-live.spl - asks the model to read secret.txt outside the sandbox and shows the runtime blocking it, returning a restriction_violation payl"},{"title":"Failures & errors","page":"Failures & errors","href":"failures.html","level":1,"text":"A failure stops the script immediately - unless you catch it. Every kind of failure shares one JSON payload, so handling code is uniform whether the cause was a bad model answer, a blocked tool call or an assert."},{"title":"The failure payload","page":"Failures & errors","href":"failures.html#payload","level":2,"text":"FieldMeaning statusAlways \"failure\". reasonHuman-readable explanation. commandThe source line, as plain text, where the failure was raised. line1-based line number. status_codeMachine-readable category - see below."},{"title":"Status codes","page":"Failures & errors","href":"failures.html#status-codes","level":2,"text":"status_codeRaised when assertion_failedAn assert condition is falsy. explicit_failureThe script executes fail '…'. invalid_formatA fmt='object' response is not valid JSON (object or array). restriction_violationA tool call is blocked by an active restrict scope. llm_errorThe backend failed: network, authentication, timeout, model error. tool_errorA tool failed: missing file, HTTP error, non-zero s"},{"title":"try / except","page":"Failures & errors","href":"failures.html#try-except","level":2,"text":"Handle failures with try and except name. The caught payload is bound to name as a dict. try without except is a syntax error. Any enforce/forbid and restrict scopes opened inside the try block are unwound before the handler runs. ℹ️ Not everything is catchable Syntax errors are found before the script starts and are never catchable. Everything raised while running - including runtime errors - is."},{"title":"assert, fail, success","page":"Failures & errors","href":"failures.html#assert-fail-success","level":2,"text":"Three special statements control when the program stops."},{"title":"assert","page":"Failures & errors","href":"failures.html#assert","level":3,"text":"assert cond ['message'] raises assertion_failed when the condition is falsy. The message is an optional expression and interpolates like any string."},{"title":"fail","page":"Failures & errors","href":"failures.html#fail","level":3,"text":"fail reason raises explicit_failure. Use it for conditions that are not simple expressions, such as a CI gate."},{"title":"success","page":"Failures & errors","href":"failures.html#success","level":3,"text":"success [result] stops the script successfully. result is printed to stdout - strings raw, everything else as JSON - and returned by the interpreter. Without a value nothing is printed. A script that simply runs to the end also exits with code 0."},{"title":"Uncaught failures","page":"Failures & errors","href":"failures.html#uncaught","level":2,"text":"An uncaught failure prints the payload (pretty-printed JSON) to stdout and exits with code 1. Even then the variable store is saved, so state recorded before the failure survives. Syntax errors are reported on stderr as file:line:col: syntax error: message with exit code 2."},{"title":"Retry patterns","page":"Failures & errors","href":"failures.html#retry","level":2,"text":"LLM output is untrusted input. The idiom is: validate deterministically, feed the rejection reason back, ask again, and give up with fail when the budget is spent. 💡 Why this works Variance in LLM output can't be removed, but it can be contained: fmt='object' catches malformed JSON, assert catches semantically wrong JSON, and the retry loop turns both into a second chance with better instructions"},{"title":"Getting started","page":"Getting started","href":"getting-started.html","level":1,"text":"From a fresh checkout to a running script in a few minutes. Syntax checks work offline; running LLM commands needs a one-time spl login."},{"title":"Requirements","page":"Getting started","href":"getting-started.html#requirements","level":2,"text":"Node.js 20 or newer. An authenticated GitHub Copilot: run spl login once (see Authentication). Not needed for --check or for scripts without LLM commands."},{"title":"Install & build","page":"Getting started","href":"getting-started.html#install","level":2,"text":"The interpreter lives in the lang/ folder of the repository. npm install also runs prepare, which compiles the TypeScript sources to dist/. The package exposes two binaries, spl and spl-lang, both pointing at dist/cli.js."},{"title":"Your first run","page":"Getting started","href":"getting-started.html#first-run","level":2,"text":"Check the syntax. --check parses the script and exits - no LLM, no side effects. Log in to Copilot. Once per machine; it opens GitHub's device flow (a code you confirm in the browser). Run it against Copilot. The Copilot SDK is used with your login. Or use npx. From a checkout, or as npx spl-lang once published. ℹ️ stdout vs. stderr The value passed to success (or to a top-level return) goes to st"},{"title":"Write a script","page":"Getting started","href":"getting-started.html#hello","level":2,"text":"Create hello.spl: 💡 Literal braces in prompts Every string interpolates {expr}. To write a literal brace - for example a JSON template - double it ({{ }}), escape it (\\{ \\}) or use a raw string r'{\"a\": 1}'."},{"title":"Exit codes","page":"Getting started","href":"getting-started.html#exit-codes","level":2,"text":"CodeMeaning 0The script finished (reached success, a top-level return or the end). 1Uncaught failure - the failure payload is printed to stdout. 2Syntax error or command-line usage error. 70Internal interpreter error."},{"title":"Repository tour","page":"Getting started","href":"getting-started.html#repo-tour","level":2,"text":"PathContents LANG-PRD.mdProduct requirements - the language specification. lang/The interpreter: src/, tests/, examples/ and docs/syntax.ebnf. vscode/VS Code extension: TextMate grammar, snippets, language configuration, tests. docs/This documentation site."},{"title":"Run the tests","page":"Getting started","href":"getting-started.html#tests","level":2,"text":""},{"title":"Grammar (EBNF)","page":"Grammar (EBNF)","href":"grammar.html","level":1,"text":"The authoritative syntax definition, as shipped in lang/docs/syntax.ebnf. The parser is strict: anything outside this grammar is a syntax error reported with exit code 2."},{"title":"Program & statements","page":"Grammar (EBNF)","href":"grammar.html#program","level":2,"text":""},{"title":"Expressions","page":"Grammar (EBNF)","href":"grammar.html#expressions","level":2,"text":"Listed from lowest to highest precedence."},{"title":"LLM commands","page":"Grammar (EBNF)","href":"grammar.html#llm-grammar","level":2,"text":""},{"title":"Lexical rules","page":"Grammar (EBNF)","href":"grammar.html#lexical","level":2,"text":""},{"title":"Reserved words","page":"Grammar (EBNF)","href":"grammar.html#reserved","level":2,"text":"These cannot be used as variable, function or parameter names: GroupWords Declarations & flowlet def return if elif else for in while break continue pass Failure handlingtry except assert fail success Scopesenforce forbid restrict LLM & toolsthink read_file write_file web_fetch web_search shell Operators & literalsand or not True False None ℹ️ Contextual words fs, net and exec only have meaning di"},{"title":"Write programs where prompts are syntax.","page":"Write programs where prompts are syntax.","href":"index.html","level":1,"text":"SPL is a Python-like language with LLM calls built into the grammar. Everything outside the model call is plain, deterministic code - loops, arithmetic, validation, retries - and every tool the model touches is checked by the runtime."},{"title":"Why SPL?","page":"Write programs where prompts are syntax.","href":"index.html#why","level":2,"text":"Scripts that glue LLMs to files, shells and the web usually end up as a pile of string-building and ad-hoc parsing. SPL makes the interesting parts first-class and keeps the rest boring on purpose. 🧠"},{"title":"Design tenets","page":"Write programs where prompts are syntax.","href":"index.html#tenets","level":2,"text":"🎯Best-effort reproducibility Control flow, arithmetic and interpolation are fully deterministic. For LLM calls reproducibility is best effort (the Copilot SDK has no temperature or seed setting). Identical output is not guaranteed - guard with assert and fmt. 🛡️Least privilege by restriction The MVP is allow-by-default: a script has the permissions of the user running it. Authors narrow permissi"},{"title":"How a run works","page":"Write programs where prompts are syntax.","href":"index.html#how-it-works","level":2,"text":"ParseStrict lexer & parser. Unknown modifiers are syntax errors. → InterpretDeterministic evaluation of statements and expressions. → AssembleConstraints, restrictions and modifier hints are prepended to the prompt. → CallCopilot SDK. Tool calls are intercepted and checked. → ResultValue of success to stdout, logs to stderr."},{"title":"Where to next","page":"Write programs where prompts are syntax.","href":"index.html#explore","level":2,"text":"🚀"},{"title":"Non-goals","page":"Write programs where prompts are syntax.","href":"index.html#non-goals","level":2,"text":"Bit-for-bit reproducible LLM output (no record/replay or response cache in the MVP). Being a full Python implementation - only a reasonable subset is supported."},{"title":"Architecture","page":"Architecture","href":"internals.html","level":1,"text":"How the interpreter in lang/src is put together - useful if you want to embed it, add a backend or contribute."},{"title":"Execution pipeline","page":"Architecture","href":"internals.html#pipeline","level":2,"text":"lexer.tsSource to tokens, with string parts and interpolation expressions. → parser.tsRecursive descent to the typed AST. All strict checks happen here. → interpreter.tsTree-walking evaluator with scopes, constraints and restrictions. → llm.tsPrompt assembly and fmt='object' parsing. → backendcopilot.ts; tools run through tools.ts."},{"title":"Modules","page":"Architecture","href":"internals.html#modules","level":2,"text":"FileResponsibility cli.tsArgument parsing, file loading, backend/store selection, result printing and exit codes. lexer.tsTokenizer. Strings become parts (text or expr); raw strings and brace escapes handled here. parser.tsParser and strict validation of modifiers, loop-only break, let-only modification, directives. ast.tsAST types: Program, Stmt, Expr, AskMods, ThinkMods, ToolName, RestrictKind. "},{"title":"Execution model","page":"Architecture","href":"internals.html#execution-model","level":2,"text":"Environments. A chain of variable maps. The global map is seeded from the store. Each user-function call creates a child map whose parent is the function's closure, and each bare { } block a child map of the current one; let always writes to the innermost map. All other blocks reuse the current map. Control signals. break, continue and return propagate as values from block execution, not as except"},{"title":"Anatomy of an LLM call","page":"Architecture","href":"internals.html#llm-call","level":2,"text":"Evaluate the prompt (interpolation happens here, deterministically) and, for tools, the parameters. Pre-run the tool (explicit tool commands only, and only when the script supplied every parameter the tool needs) through ToolHost, which checks the active restrictions first. Otherwise the model calls the tool with the supplied parameters pinned. Build the request: assembled prompt, model, reasoning"},{"title":"Embedding","page":"Architecture","href":"internals.html#embedding","level":2,"text":"The package exports a small programmatic API from dist/index.js: ExportPurpose parse(source)Returns a Program; throws SplSyntaxError (with line, col). run(program, options)Returns { status: 'success', value, hasValue } or { status: 'failure', failure }. RunOptionsbackend, logger, store?, cwd?, model?, maxCallDepth?. CopilotBackendThe backend (GitHub Copilot SDK). login, logout, authStatusCopilot a"},{"title":"Extension points","page":"Architecture","href":"internals.html#extending","level":2,"text":""},{"title":"Custom backend","page":"Architecture","href":"internals.html#backend-interface","level":3,"text":"Implement LlmBackend to target another model provider:"},{"title":"Testing scripts","page":"Architecture","href":"internals.html#live-testing","level":3,"text":"There is no mock backend: scripts that need an LLM are tested directly on GitHub Copilot. tests/live.ts wraps the real CopilotBackend in a recorder, so a test runs against the real service and can still assert on the assembled prompts (backend.calls[i].prompt). Keep assertions about structure, not wording. Live tests are skipped when the runtime is not authenticated (spl login). Tests that must no"},{"title":"Test suite","page":"Architecture","href":"internals.html#tests","level":2,"text":"FileCovers tests/parser.test.tsGrammar, strict modifier validation, scoping and let syntax, syntax errors. tests/interpreter.test.tsDeterministic language, scoping, return, failures, restrict enforcement, variable store (no LLM). tests/permissions.test.tsThe Copilot permission layer (hard enforcement) and network restrictions. tests/tools.test.tsParsing of web_search results. tests/live.test.tsLLM"},{"title":"Tech stack","page":"Architecture","href":"internals.html#stack","level":2,"text":"TypeScript (ESM) targeting Node 20+. @github/copilot-sdk as the only runtime dependency. Runnable with npx without a prior global install."},{"title":"Language basics","page":"Language basics","href":"language.html","level":1,"text":"The deterministic half of SPL: a Python-flavoured language with braces. Nothing on this page calls a model - given the same inputs it always produces the same results."},{"title":"Program structure","page":"Language basics","href":"language.html#structure","level":2,"text":"A script is an optional @{…} directive header followed by statements. # starts a comment that runs to the end of the line."},{"title":"Directives","page":"Language basics","href":"language.html#directives","level":3,"text":"The only directive in the MVP is model. Its value must be a plain string (no interpolation). The --model CLI option takes precedence over the directive."},{"title":"Variables","page":"Language basics","href":"language.html#variables","level":2,"text":"let declares a variable and is how you change it: every modification starts with let, including compound operators (+= -= *= /= //= %=) and member or item targets. A bare x = 5 is a syntax error. Reading an unknown name is a runtime error."},{"title":"Scope","page":"Language basics","href":"language.html#scope","level":3,"text":"let name = … binds in the current scope. Two constructs open a new scope: a bare { … } block and a function body (with its parameters). A let there shadows an outer variable of the same name and leaves it untouched; the shadow disappears at the closing brace. if/elif/else, for, while, try/except, enforce, forbid and restrict blocks do not open a scope: a let inside them updates the enclosing varia"},{"title":"Persistence","page":"Language basics","href":"language.html#persistence","level":3,"text":"Top-level variables can be saved between runs with the variable store (--store). See CLI & runtime."},{"title":"Types & values","page":"Language basics","href":"language.html#types","level":2,"text":"type()LiteralsNotes 'None'NoneFalsy. 'bool'True FalsePython spelling. 'number'42 3.14 1e-3One numeric type. int() truncates, float() parses. 'str''a' \"a\" '''a''' r'a'Indexed by Unicode character. 'list'[1, 2, 3]Mutable, trailing comma allowed. 'dict'{'a': 1}Keys must be strings. Insertion ordered. Also readable as d.a. 'function'def f(x) { … }First-class values. ⚠️ type() returns a string Compare "},{"title":"Truthiness","page":"Language basics","href":"language.html#truthiness","level":3,"text":"None, False, 0, empty strings, empty lists and empty dicts are falsy. Everything else (including functions) is truthy."},{"title":"Strings & interpolation","page":"Language basics","href":"language.html#strings","level":2,"text":"All four quote styles interpolate {expression}. Any expression is allowed, including calls and indexing. Substitution is as is: strings raw, True/False/None in Python spelling, lists and dicts as JSON."},{"title":"Quote styles & escapes","page":"Language basics","href":"language.html#quotes","level":3,"text":"FormBehaviour '…' \"…\"Single line; escapes and interpolation. '''…''' \"\"\"…\"\"\"May span lines; escapes and interpolation. Ideal for prompts. r'…'Raw: no escapes, no interpolation. Valid escapes: \\n \\t \\r \\0 \\\\ \\' \\\" \\{ \\}. Anything else is a syntax error, and so is a stray single } in a string - write }} or \\}."},{"title":"Operators","page":"Language basics","href":"language.html#operators","level":2,"text":"Lowest to highest precedence: OperatorsDescription x if cond else yConditional expression. orLogical or (short-circuit). andLogical and (short-circuit). notLogical negation. == != < <= > >= in not inComparison and membership. == is deep for lists and dicts. Ordering works on numbers, strings and lists. + -Add / subtract. + also joins two strings or two lists - mixing types is an error (use interpo"},{"title":"Control flow","page":"Language basics","href":"language.html#control-flow","level":2,"text":""},{"title":"if / elif / else","page":"Language basics","href":"language.html#if","level":3,"text":""},{"title":"for & while","page":"Language basics","href":"language.html#loops","level":3,"text":"for iterates lists, strings (characters) and dicts (keys). Several names unpack list items, which pairs well with items() and enumerate(). break, continue and pass work as in Python."},{"title":"Functions","page":"Language basics","href":"language.html#functions","level":2,"text":"Define with def. Functions take positional arguments only (the count is checked), return None unless return is used, can recurse (up to a maximum call depth of 200) and close over their defining scope. return leaves the innermost function immediately, from inside any nested block, loop, try or constraint block; at the top level of a script it ends the script like success, optionally with a value. "},{"title":"Built-ins","page":"Language basics","href":"language.html#builtins-brief","level":2,"text":"A compact standard library is available without imports: len, range, sorted, json_parse, log, and Python-style methods on strings, lists and dicts. The full list is on the Built-ins page."},{"title":"LLM commands","page":"LLM commands","href":"llm-commands.html","level":1,"text":"Commands are the non-deterministic part of SPL. Each one is an expression that sends a prompt to the model (or runs a tool first) and evaluates to the answer, so you can assign it, test it, or pass it to a function."},{"title":"Operational modifiers","page":"LLM commands","href":"llm-commands.html#modifiers","level":2,"text":"Every command has sensible defaults and the braces are optional: ?'q', think 'q' and read_file 'what to do' all run as they are. A brace list of modifiers directly after the command name overrides its mode: Names and values are validated by the parser against a fixed schema per command. Unknown modifiers or values are a syntax error, not silently ignored. Each modifier may appear at most once. Str"},{"title":"Ask: ?","page":"LLM commands","href":"llm-commands.html#ask","level":2,"text":"? is a prefix operator at unary precedence. The operand is the prompt - a postfix expression, so ?'a' + b means (?'a') + b and -?'n' means -(?'n'). ModifierValuesEffect len'word' 'sentence' 'paragraph' 'long'A hint prepended to the query: “Answer with a single word.”, “Answer in a single sentence.”, “Answer in a single paragraph.”, “Give a long, detailed answer.” A suggestion to the model, not a h"},{"title":"Structured output with fmt='object'","page":"LLM commands","href":"llm-commands.html#fmt-object","level":3,"text":"The parser accepts a bare JSON object or array, JSON wrapped in a Markdown code fence, or JSON embedded in surrounding prose. Scalars, or anything unparseable, raise a failure with status_code='invalid_format', which you can catch and retry (see retry patterns). 💡 Escaping JSON templates Inside a prompt, double the braces of a JSON template: {{\"a\": 1}} produces {\"a\": 1}. Single braces are interpo"},{"title":"Think: think","page":"LLM commands","href":"llm-commands.html#think","level":2,"text":"Tells the model to deliberate about the problem before answering, and lets you choose how hard. ModifierValuesEffect level'low' 'medium' 'high'Mapped to the Copilot SDK reasoning effort. If the selected model does not support it, a warning is logged and the model default is used. showTrue False (default)When True, the model's reasoning stream is written to the log (stderr). A common idiom is a two"},{"title":"Enforce & forbid","page":"LLM commands","href":"llm-commands.html#constraints","level":2,"text":"enforce and forbid take a rule and a block. The rule is in force for every LLM call inside the block, including nested blocks and functions called from it. The runtime keeps the active rules on a stack and prepends them to each query. Both rules above are sent as: ⚠️ Guidance, not enforcement Constraints are instructions to the model; the runtime cannot verify that the answer obeys them. Use asser"},{"title":"Tool calls","page":"LLM commands","href":"llm-commands.html#tools","level":2,"text":"A tool call has the form tool{param=value} 'instructions', and the braces are optional: read_file 'Read notes.txt and summarize it'. In the MVP the tools are a fixed built-in set; user-defined tools and MCP servers are out of scope. ToolParameters (all optional)PurposeGoverned by read_filepathRead a filerestrict fs write_filepath, contentCreate or modify a filerestrict fs web_fetchurlFetch a URL ("},{"title":"How a tool call runs","page":"LLM commands","href":"llm-commands.html#tool-flow","level":3,"text":"Which side calls the tool depends on what the script supplies. When every parameter the tool needs is given (path for read_file, path and content for write_file, url, query, command), the runtime runs it: 1 &middot; RunThe runtime executes the tool with your parameters after checking restrictions. → 2 &middot; WrapThe output goes into the prompt inside <tool_output> markers, followed by your instr"},{"title":"write_file: two modes","page":"LLM commands","href":"llm-commands.html#write-file","level":3,"text":"With content deterministic The script supplies the bytes; the runtime writes them; the model only confirms. Without content LLM writes The model produces the content and writes the file itself - but the path is pinned by the script."},{"title":"Tools the model may use","page":"LLM commands","href":"llm-commands.html#llm-tools","level":3,"text":"Inside any ?, think or tool call, the model itself may use these same five tools. Every call it makes goes through the same interception and is checked against the active restrict scopes. Any other Copilot permission request is rejected."},{"title":"How the final prompt is built","page":"LLM commands","href":"llm-commands.html#prompt-assembly","level":2,"text":"Before each call the runtime assembles sections in a fixed order, separated by blank lines: Rules from active enforce / forbid blocks. Active restrictions, described in plain language. For think: “Think carefully about the problem before giving the final answer.” The len hint. For fmt='object': an instruction to reply with ONLY valid JSON. Your prompt. Every non-silent log level (error, info, debu"},{"title":"Restrictions","page":"Restrictions","href":"restrictions.html","level":1,"text":"restrict narrows what a block - and the LLM tools it invokes - may do. Without it, everything is allowed: a script has the full permissions of the user running the interpreter."},{"title":"The three forms","page":"Restrictions","href":"restrictions.html#forms","level":2,"text":"FormEffect restrict './target/out' { … }Reads and writes are allowed only inside the folder. Same as restrict fs. restrict net 'api.example.com' { … }Network access is allowed only to the listed hosts. restrict exec 'npm,git' { … }Shell commands are allowed only for the listed executables. The argument is an expression evaluating to a comma-separated string ('npm,git') or a list of strings. An emp"},{"title":"Two layers of enforcement","page":"Restrictions","href":"restrictions.html#enforcement","level":2,"text":"🗣️1 · GuidanceThe runtime tells the model about the active restrictions in the prompt, so it plans within them. 🚧2 · Hard enforcementThe runtime intercepts every file, network and exec call and blocks any that violate the active scopes. A blocked call raises a restriction_violation failure. It is catchable with try/except:"},{"title":"Nesting","page":"Restrictions","href":"restrictions.html#nesting","level":2,"text":"Restrictions are scoped to their block and can be nested. Nested restrictions of the same kind intersect: they can only narrow the allowed set, never widen it. Different kinds combine freely."},{"title":"Matching rules","page":"Restrictions","href":"restrictions.html#rules","level":2,"text":""},{"title":"File system","page":"Restrictions","href":"restrictions.html#fs","level":3,"text":"Paths are canonicalized before checking: made absolute relative to --cwd, .. removed, symbolic links resolved - including dangling links, whose targets are followed so a write cannot escape through them. A path is allowed when it is the allowed folder itself or inside it. On Windows the comparison is case-insensitive."},{"title":"Network","page":"Restrictions","href":"restrictions.html#net","level":3,"text":"net matches exact hostnames and *.example.com wildcards. Entries may be bare hosts or URLs (the host is extracted; ports are ignored). Only http: and https: URLs are fetched. Redirects are followed manually (at most 5) and the host is re-checked on every hop. web_search is network access too: it queries html.duckduckgo.com, so a restrict net scope must list that host for searches to work."},{"title":"Executables","page":"Restrictions","href":"restrictions.html#exec","level":3,"text":"exec matches the executable name. On Windows .exe, .cmd, .bat and .com suffixes are ignored. Inside a restrict exec block, commands must be named without a path (git, not /usr/bin/git). While any restriction is active, shell accepts only simple commands: no & | ; < >, redirects, substitution, chaining or embedded double quotes, so the check is exact. Path-like and URL arguments are also checked ag"},{"title":"What is covered","page":"Restrictions","href":"restrictions.html#what-is-guarded","level":2,"text":"CallerChecked? Explicit tool call in a script (read_file{…}, shell{…}, …)yes Tool calls the model makes during ? / think / tool commandsyes Any other Copilot permission requestrejected Plain SPL code (arithmetic, log, variables)nothing to restrict 🔮 Future direction A deny-by-default permission model is a possible future change. The MVP is allow-by-default."}];
